From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MIRAGE <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 15:47:43 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] MIRAGE: a session's state, and file commands that answer as
 GNU's do

Three things that go together, because each needs the others to be true.

Session state (cowrie/mirage_state.py). A journal of what a session changed:
every cowrie.fs.change, changes of directory and environment, and processes
an answer started. An applier for operations decided outside the emulator (a
responder's answer, a file the policy plants): their shape is checked, then
each against this session's own tree in order, and only then are the
mutators an emulated command uses called, all of an answer's operations or
none. A view of the paths a command names, as the session's user could see
them. The vocabulary is the gate contract's; test/mirage_ops_cases.json is
shared with it so the two implementations give the same verdicts.

The tree reports every change. The journal's replay test found that cp
copied nodes without telling the filesystem, rmdir went round its mutator,
and there was no way to make a link. fs.symlink and fs.hardlink exist now,
and every command below changes the tree through a mutator that reports.

File commands, held to a recording of the reference host
(test/mirage_file_reference.jsonl: 105 command lines, compared byte for byte
on stdout and stderr). mkdir, rmdir, cp, mv, rm, touch, ln, chmod, chown,
chgrp, tee, find, cut, xargs, readlink, realpath, basename, dirname, the
checksum programs, seq, tac, rev, nl, printenv and mktemp are rewritten or
new. Stock Cowrie's mkdir made a directory called -p; its redirection
replaced a directory with a file (echo x > /tmp); chown did nothing; find
knew three tests; cut did not cut characters; and a user who was not root
could write anywhere. The modes are enforced now, by the same checks the
applier uses.

The checksum programs answer for the image's own programs from a table of
their real checksums ([mirage] file_hashes) and decline where there is none:
the emulated tree does not hold those bytes, and a made-up checksum of
/bin/ls is checked against Debian's in one line.
